[size=14pt][b][ANN] BlockYard 0.1.5 — ships in Simple mode, the mempool pushed live, and GitHub is now the only home[/b][/size]

[b]Repository:[/b] [url]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard[/url]
[b]Release:[/b] [url]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard/releases/tag/v0.1.5[/url]
[b]Install:[/b] [tt]git clone https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard.git[/tt] — npm is retired (see below)
[b]Licence:[/b] Apache-2.0

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/10/08/Ul5QS9.jpg[/img]

BlockYard 0.1.5 is out, two weeks after 0.1.3 and carrying 0.1.4's security work with it. The headline is a change of default: [b]BlockYard now ships in Simple mode.[/b] A fresh install draws its 3D boards with a new renderer that keeps the cubes, the camera and the hover, and drops everything that repaints on its own. A board at rest paints no frames at all, so a monitor left open all day on a laptop, a tablet on the wall or a thin client costs the graphics card nothing.

[b]But there is a lot of visual fun to discover if you want to go exploring.[/b] Open Settings (the gear), Appearance, "3D renderer", and pick [b]WebGL[/b] (or [b]Software[/b] on a machine without a good graphics card). Everything from the last releases comes back: the Galaxy, the Formation, the Sun and the Earth as skies; thirty-odd idle effects; blocks that lift, fly their lanes and bounce into place when a block is found; neon, chrome and satin finishes; and the price board's black hole, supernova and ball lightning. The switch is live and needs no reload. Go and have a look.

If you are new here: BlockYard is a self-hosted web app you point at [b]your own Bitcoin node[/b] (Bitcoin Core, or Bitcoin Machine Code). You get a live dashboard of the node and the network, a 3D view of the block space being built in the mempool, a block, transaction and address explorer with its own address index, a mining dashboard, opt-in exchange prices, a kiosk view, and some DOS games. It is plain JavaScript on Node.js with [b]zero dependencies[/b] and [b]zero telemetry[/b], it only reads from your node, and it ships hardened: bound to this machine, sign-in on, and HTTPS with a certificate it makes itself.

[size=12pt][b]Simple mode, and why it is the default[/b][/size]

The skies and effects were built on a big desktop graphics card, and on a Mac laptop the monitor kept the GPU working at half its capacity even with every board parked. Measuring it in Chromium turned up two separate costs. One was the boards. The other was the page itself: the live dot, the block meter's edge and the overdue warning were endless CSS animations, and any endless animation makes the browser redraw the whole page at every refresh of the screen.

Simple fixes both. It draws the same cubes under the same oblique camera, flat: no sky animation, no effects, no shadows or finishes, and no flight between layouts. It paints when the data or the pointer changes and not otherwise, and the page's pulses hold still while it is chosen. Measured on the live monitor, composited frames in ten seconds: Overview went from 600 to 6. Behind the board is a still star field, drawn once.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/10/08/Ul5G3N.jpg[/img]

Software and WebGL got lighter too, for anyone who switches. The page's pulses now [i]tick[/i] (one attribute flipped every 1.3 seconds) instead of animating, which took Software from 1,206 composited frames in ten seconds to 426. WebGL draws at one pixel per CSS pixel by default, which cut the GPU process's busy time by a third on a high-density screen. There is also a frame-rate cap, 60 by default.

