[size=12pt][b]The mempool, pushed[/b][/size]

The pool boards (Overview, Block space, Mining, Kiosk) used to follow a 30-second poll. Now the server keeps the node's mempool in memory between full reads and applies its changes as they happen. Where the node publishes ZMQ [tt]sequence[/tt] notifications (bmc does), BlockYard subscribes; the ZMQ protocol (ZMTP) is written here, so there is still no dependency. Otherwise it compares the node's transaction ids every 5 seconds. Every 5 seconds, if anything changed, the board's picture is rebuilt and sent over the page's existing live stream. The full read (47 MB and 0.6 s on Core, with 77,525 transactions) now runs every ten minutes as a check, and logs how far the kept copy had drifted.

The boards keep their layout between updates, so an update is a handful of arrivals and departures rather than a whole new board. On WebGL and Software these play as a short choreography: departures fly off, arrivals fall and bounce into their squares, and the board is still again in about three and a half seconds. A found block still re-packs the board, and there are now flights of 1, 2, 3, 5, 10 and 20 seconds to pick from, or None. "Refresh every" sets how often a board takes a new picture, from 5 seconds to 2 minutes.

[size=12pt][b]The Detailed board is Core's block[/b][/size]

The Detailed view used to show the mempool sorted by each transaction's own fee rate and cut at 1,000,000 vB. That put a child paying for its parent (CPFP) where its own rate said, and left the parent out: on Core's pool on 1 October, 316 of about 3,600 transactions on the board would not have been in Core's block, and 317 of Core's were missing. Now the board is the block the "being built" card shows: Core's chunk fee rates, each package kept whole, filled the way Core fills the end of a block. On the same pool it came within 5 transactions and 0.06% in fees of the node's own [tt]getblocktemplate[/tt]. It is assembled in about 60 ms instead of 260–400, and its scale is fitted to within 0.2%.

[img width=800]https://talkimg.com/images/2026/10/08/Ul5npw.jpg[/img]

[size=12pt][b]Security: a fifth AI audit (0.1.4)[/b][/size]

0.1.4 never got its own post here, so: a fifth AI security audit, brutally honest by request, with the DOS Diversions' x86 interpreter read hardest of all. It came back as the best-defended part of the codebase. The audit found 15 items (1 high, 4 medium, 10 low or informational), and each was fixed the same day against a regression test that failed first. The headline fix: the RPC transport itself now refuses a wallet method that has no explicit authorization, not only the console route in front of it, so no future code path can reach a wallet method by skipping a check. The audit trail is now hash-chained ([tt]npm run verify-audit[/tt] checks it), and login has the same cross-site protection as every other route that changes state. The full report is [tt]docs/SECURITY-AUDIT-2026-09-22.md[/tt].

[size=12pt][b]Smaller things[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Quality flags clear when their cause does.[/b] A healthy node's page was showing five warnings, none of them current: RPC timeouts counted since start-up rather than in the last ten minutes, a stale-tip warning that never cleared, a handshake warning raised on the first failure and kept for ever. Each one now goes away when its cause does.
[*][b]A restart takes about 2 seconds, not 45.[/b] The server used to keep accepting connections until the very end of its shutdown, so a page reconnecting at that moment held it open until systemd killed it.
[*][b]The Kiosk no longer stutters[/b] beside a Detailed board, and effects on the Detailed board cost 20–36% less on WebGL.
[*][b]Bitcoin Machine Code:[/b] a Dial handoff card on the node page, and the node's RPC lock is named when it stalls.
[*][tt]scripts/manage-users.js[/tt] reads a piped password.
[/list]

[size=12pt][b]npm is retired: GitHub is the one place to get BlockYard[/b][/size]

Keeping a second release channel in step was more trouble than it was worth. npm will not let a version be republished, so a docs fix after release left the two disagreeing, and publishing needed a 2FA dance from another machine. So from 0.1.5 on, [b]GitHub is the only authoritative source[/b] for BlockYard. The npm package stops at 0.1.3 and is marked deprecated, and the repository is set so it cannot be published there by accident. A checkout is also the better install: [tt]git pull[/tt] updates it.

[b]Installed from npm?[/b] Your config, accounts and address index in [tt]~/.blockyard[/tt] carry over as they are. The same command is in the checkout and reads the same place:

[code]
git clone https://github.com/BobClawblaw/blockyard.git
cd blockyard
node bin/blockyard.js start
npm uninstall -g blockyard
[/code]

Under systemd, point [tt]ExecStart[/tt] at [tt]node <checkout>/bin/blockyard.js start[/tt]. [tt]docs/INSTALL.md[/tt] §2 has the details.

[size=12pt][b]Upgrading[/b][/size]

[tt]git pull[/tt], then restart.

[list]
[*][b]Your settings are kept.[/b] Simple is the default only for settings nobody has saved. If you had already saved Display settings, your renderer stays as it was; and if you want Simple, it is in Settings, Appearance.
[*][b]Nothing to migrate.[/b] No setting was renamed and no file moved.
[/list]

[size=12pt][b]How it was made[/b][/size]

As before, [b]every line was written by an AI, directed by a human[/b], including the fixes, the tests and this post. The operator decided what mattered, what was wrong and what looked bad, and typed none of it. It is still [b]experimental pre-release software[/b]. Expect bugs, and tell us what broke.

